# Constraints

- NEVER allow tenant_id to be supplied by the end-user client in any form (query param, body field, header). Tenant identity is always server-resolved.
- NEVER use a single shared PostgreSQL role for all tenants in schema-per-tenant mode — create per-tenant roles where data sensitivity requires it.
- NEVER cache tenant data without an explicit invalidation path. Stale tenant config (e.g., pointing to a deleted tenant) is a security risk.
- NEVER run DDL migrations on tenant schemas during peak traffic without a maintenance window or online-DDL tooling (pg_repack, pgroll).
- ALL tenant-scoped database models MUST inherit from a base class that enforces tenant filtering. Raw SQL queries must go through a tenant-aware query builder.
- NEVER expose internal tenant IDs (UUIDs or integers) in URLs or API responses to end-users. Use slugs or opaque identifiers.
- Connection pool size MUST account for (num_tenants × connections_per_tenant) in schema-per-tenant mode. Monitor with pgbouncer or PgPool-II.

